WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:00.049 --> 00:00:02.560
Hi, everyone! Dan for BBC Learning English here.

00:00:02.560 --> 00:00:03.840
Today we're going to talk about

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:06.600
'don't mind' and 'doesn't matter'.

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:10.080
The verb 'mind' means 'dislike, be annoyed by

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:11.640
or object to'.

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:14.280
It's followed by (verb)ing and often used in

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:16.720
negatives and questions.

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:19.400
For example: Do you mind opening the window?

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:21.480
Sam: No, I don't mind.

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:23.340
Dan: If someone says 'I don't mind',

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:25.300
it means that they have no preference

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:27.900
or that they are happy for something to happen.

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:30.780
However, the verb 'matter' in English can mean

00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:32.920
'be important'.

00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:35.200
'English matters,' means

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:37.140
'English is important'.

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:39.520
If we say 'it doesn't matter',

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:41.580
it means that the thing that we are talking about

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:44.180
is not important or not significant.

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:46.100
Do you want tea or coffee?

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:47.240
Sam: It doesn't matter.

00:00:47.240 --> 00:00:47.980
Dan: OK!

00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:51.120
Sometimes they can both mean the same thing.

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:52.920
Sam: Do you want chicken for dinner?

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:54.020
Dan: I don't mind!

00:00:54.020 --> 00:00:55.700
Sam:  Do you want chicken for dinner?

00:00:55.700 --> 00:00:56.900
Dan: It doesn't matter to me.

